<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Cynetes</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 150 BCE (approx.) · capital Conistorgis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The people of the Algarve, named by Herodotus as the westernmost inhabitants of Europe and linked to the enigmatic south-western script.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Cynetes, or Conii, lived in the far south of modern Portugal, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a> and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alentejo">Alentejo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> in the 5th century BCE called them the most westerly people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>. Their lands lay on the Atlantic routes that connected <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tartessos">Tartessos</a> with the north.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their region holds most of the inscribed stone stelae in the south-western script, the oldest writing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, dated to the 7th to 5th centuries BCE; the language they record is undeciphered and debated. Little is known of their political organisation, though Appian names a town, Conistorgis, as their chief place.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 153 BCE <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lusitanians">Lusitanians</a> raiding Roman territory captured Conistorgis, then held by allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The Cynetes were absorbed into the Roman province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania</a>, and their name disappears from the record.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cynetes.jpg" alt="Cynetes" width="320" height="298"><figcaption>Image: The Ogre · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethnographic_Iberia_200_BCE.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 150 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Conistorgis</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Conii, Cunetes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-425&amp;focus=cynetes" aria-label="Cynetes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4472 763 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cynetes lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4792 1019h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4792 1019h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Portugal. <a href="/atlas?year=-425&amp;focus=cynetes">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
